---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import focus from "../data/focus.json";
const M = focus.Mazza;
const spine = M.groups.filter((g) => g.onSpine.length);
const apart = M.groups.filter((g) => !g.onSpine.length);
const big = apart.reduce((a, g) => a + g.n, 0);
---
<Base title="The Mazza" eyebrow={`${M.total} bearers, ${M.groups.length} descent groups`}
      dek="The surname this archive is named for is also the most fragmented thing in it. A hundred and twenty Mazza sit in six groups that no record joins to each other.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Piedimonte Etneo — 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The marriage, and then the birth: two acts close the deepest Sicilian generation</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The parish register was the wrong door. It was browsed to the right year and it held
      nothing, and the reason turned out to be simple enough to be embarrassing: <strong>civil
      registration began in the south in 1866</strong>, so a marriage of about 1870 is a
      <em>civil</em> act, kept by the comune and now on the Antenati portal — and the civil act is
      the one that names all four parents.</p>
      <p>The annual index of the Piedimonte Etneo marriages of 1871 gives, in a clerk's hand,
      <strong>Mazza Rosario / Bonanno Leonarda — numero di registro 14</strong>. Here is the act.</p>

      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">Married</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>30 April 1871</strong>, four in the afternoon, in the
        <em>casa comunale</em> of Piedimonte Etneo, before Barone Mariano, <em>sindaco</em></div>
        <div class="w">the tree has no marriage</div>
        <div class="k">The groom</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Rosario Mazza</strong> — <em>celibe</em>, born and living there, a
        <strong><em>bracciante</em></strong>, entered as twenty-six</div><div class="w">new</div>
        <div class="k">His parents</div>
        <div class="v"><em>figlio di</em> <strong>Mariano</strong> <em>e di</em>
        <strong>Maria Catalano</strong></div><div class="w">confirms 1924</div>
        <div class="k">The bride</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Leonarda Bonanno</strong> — <em>celibe</em>, born and living there,
        also a <strong><em>bracciante</em></strong>, entered as twenty-six</div>
        <div class="w">a trade the tree does not give her</div>
        <div class="k">Her parents</div>
        <div class="v"><em>figlia di</em> <strong>Gaetano</strong> <em>e d'</em><strong>Anna
        Cavallaro</strong></div><div class="w">named for the first time</div>
        <div class="k">Witnesses</div>
        <div class="v">Salvatore Greco, 38, <em>calzolaio</em>; Antonino Di Lava <em>fu</em> Giuseppe,
        46, <em>bracciante</em></div><div class="w">new</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem" class="small">Piedimonte Etneo, Matrimoni 1871, atto 14 — Antenati
      <code>an_ua83392</code>, images 13–14. Act 15 follows at half past four the same afternoon; the
      registrar was running a set of weddings that day.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Anna Cavallaro, and a generation the tree
      does not have</h3>
      <p>Leonarda Bonanno has stood in this archive as a name with a father's first name attached —
      <em>daughter of Gaetano</em>, taken from a marriage proclamation of 1910. The act of 1871 gives
      her <strong>both</strong> parents: <strong>Gaetano Bonanno</strong> and <strong>Anna
      Cavallaro</strong>. They are the Bonanno equal of Mariano Mazza and Maria Catalano, and like
      them they are <strong>entirely absent from the family tree</strong>.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">And then his birth, to the day</h3>
      <p>With the parents fixed, the birth was worth hunting. The Piedimonte birth registers run from
      <strong>1820</strong> on Antenati, and every volume ends with a table of the whole year's
      births in act order, giving the child, the comune, <strong>both parents</strong> and the day —
      six openings for a year. The table for 1845, at entry <strong>64</strong>, reads:
      <strong>Mazza Rosario — Piedimonte — Mazza Mariano / Catalano Maria</strong>.</p>

      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">Born</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>2 June 1845</strong>, eleven in the morning, <em>nella casa di loro
        abitazione</em></div><div class="w">the tree says 1850</div>
        <div class="k">Declared by</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Mariano Mazza</strong>, <strong>aged thirty-six</strong>, of
        profession <strong><em>bracciale</em></strong>, before Antonino Grassi, <em>sindaco</em></div>
        <div class="w">so born c. 1809</div>
        <div class="k">Mother</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Maria Catalano</strong>, <strong>aged thirty-six</strong></div>
        <div class="w">so born c. 1809</div>
        <div class="k">Baptised</div>
        <div class="v">the same day, <strong>2 June 1845</strong> — the <em>parroco</em> of the
        Chiesa Maggiore returned the notice at once</div><div class="w">new</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem" class="small">Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1845, atto 64 — Antenati
      <code>an_ua72387</code>, image 66. Comune di Piedimonte, distretto di Acireale, Provincia di
      Catania.</p>

      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Put the three acts side by side and the deepest Sicilian
      generation is no longer an inference from anything. <strong>Mariano Mazza and Maria
      Catalano</strong> are named as Rosario's parents in his <strong>birth act of 1845</strong>, in
      his <strong>marriage act of 1871</strong>, and in his <strong>death act of 1924</strong> —
      three registers, seventy-nine years apart, one couple. The birth act also does what neither of
      the others could: it dates <em>them</em>, both at thirty-six in June 1845, so both born about
      <strong>1809</strong>.</p>
      <p>One honest note. The marriage act enters Rosario as <em>d'anni ventisei</em> — twenty-six —
      and his birth act makes him twenty-five and eleven months on the day. Declared ages in these
      registers are routinely a year out; the birth act is the one to believe.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">What is now cheap that was not</h3>
      <p>Fifty volumes of Piedimonte births, <strong>1820 to 1863</strong>, are now written down with
      their arks. Because each year's table lists <strong>both parents</strong> and not only the
      child, it is a finding aid for a <em>couple</em>: reading the parents column down a year finds
      every child Mariano Mazza and Maria Catalano had in it. Rosario's brothers and sisters, and the
      generation above Mariano, are a sweep away rather than a search.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Sicily, at last — 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rosario Mazza's death act, and the name line stops being an inference</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The Mazza are this archive's namesake and its oldest unfinished business. The Calabrian work
      ran hot for a fortnight while <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo/")}>Piedimonte Etneo</a> sat
      untouched. One afternoon on FamilySearch put that right.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">Died</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>15 November 1924</strong> at <strong>Presa, Piedimonte Etneo</strong>,
        aged <strong>seventy-nine</strong> — certificate 15</div><div class="w">the tree has no death</div>
        <div class="k">Born</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>1845</strong>, Piedimonte Etneo. The tree says <strong>1850</strong>.</div>
        <div class="w">five years</div>
        <div class="k">Trade</div>
        <div class="v"><strong><em>Contadino</em></strong> — a peasant farmer</div>
        <div class="w">new</div>
        <div class="k">Father</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Mariano</strong></div><div class="w">named</div>
        <div class="k">Mother</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Maria Catalano</strong></div><div class="w">named</div>
        <div class="k">Wife</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Leonarda Bonanno</strong></div><div class="w">confirmed</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>The 1845 birth is not a surprise — it is a vindication.</strong>
      This archive derived 1845 from the ages the marriage proclamations gave Rosario in 1898, 1902
      and 1910, against a family tree that says 1850, and said so. The death act agrees.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">And the name line stops being an inference</h3>
      <p>The archive reached <strong>Mariano Mazza and Maria Catalano</strong> the hard way: by
      reading <em>"di Mariano"</em> off marriage proclamations and assembling a generation nobody had
      claimed. It was careful work and it was still an inference.</p>
      <p><strong>Rosario's own death act names both of them outright.</strong> The deepest Sicilian
      generation in this archive is now a documented fact rather than a reconstruction.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>And there is a son nobody had.</strong> The
      Acireale diocesan registers name Rosario Mazza and Leonarda Bonanno as the parents of a
      <strong>Marianum Mazza</strong> — a son called for his paternal grandfather, exactly as Sicilian
      custom predicts of a first son. He is not in the family tree.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>The registers spell his father four different ways</strong>,
      which is the practical lesson in them:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">Rogerius Mazza</div><div class="v">son <strong>Salvator</strong></div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">Rosarius Mazza</div><div class="v">son <strong>Horatius</strong> — Orazio</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">Joannes Mazza</div><div class="v">with <em>Leonora</em> Bonanno, son
        <strong>Joannem</strong> — Giovanni</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">Gaspare Mazza</div><div class="v">son <strong>SANTO</strong></div>
        <div class="w">the missing son</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The last is the one that matters. <strong>Santo Mazza is one of the
      two sons this archive found missing from the family tree</strong> — and here he is in a parish
      record, under a father's name that reads <em>Gaspare</em>. <strong>In these registers the given
      name is unreliable and the wife's name is the stable handle.</strong></p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">The register browsed, and the range pinned</h3>
      <p>The parish marriage register was then browsed by hand. <strong>The act is not found yet — but
      it is now cornered.</strong> Film 007490025 runs to 2,976 images, and reading pages gives the
      calibration:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">image 919</div><div class="v"><strong>1898</strong> — Salvatore Mazza's
        marriage</div><div class="w">known</div>
        <div class="k">image 849</div><div class="v"><strong>1888</strong> — entries 65–70</div>
        <div class="w">read</div>
        <div class="k">image 723</div><div class="v"><strong>late 1869</strong> — entries 74–79,
        <em>Die 22 Novembris 1869</em></div><div class="w">read</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">That is about <strong>seven images to the year</strong>, so 1870
      opens around <strong>image 724–726</strong> and the marriage — which their eldest child dates to
      about 1870–72 — should lie in <strong>images 724 to 745</strong>. Twenty openings, and the next
      person to sit down with this does not have to start from 1777.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Notes for whoever continues.</strong> The
      <code>?i=</code> parameter does <em>not</em> move the viewer — it reverts; the ark in the URL is
      what identifies an image. The "Enter Image number" box does work, but it commits on click or
      blur rather than on Enter, and the URL lags behind the viewer. And the fast way to scan is not
      the Latin page at all — it is the <strong>AI-extracted image index</strong> below the viewer,
      which tabulates Name, Father's Name and Mother's Name for every entry on the opening.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Why "Rosario" finds nothing in the parish
      register</h3>
      <p>The Piedimonte Etneo parish marriage register runs <strong>1777–1911</strong> and is in the
      full-text corpus — so Rosario and Leonarda's own marriage, about 1870, ought to be reachable.
      <strong>It is not, and the reason is worth recording.</strong></p>
      <p>These registers are in <strong>Latin</strong>. Salvatore's 1898 parish marriage reads
      <em>Salvator Mazza</em>, son of <strong><em>Rogerius</em> Mazza</strong> and
      <em>Leonarda Bonanno</em>. <strong>Rosario is Rogerius.</strong> And even searching that form
      returns nothing, because the OCR of the hand is so poor that the page text is close to
      unusable — what actually works on these images is the <strong>AI-extracted household
      index</strong>, not the transcript.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Written before the act was found, and left
      standing.</strong> This section ended by saying the marriage was "very probably there" in the
      parish film and would have to be browsed. It was browsed — twenty openings of it — and it was
      not there. The film's own index holds no marriage for the pair at all. The act was in the
      <em>civil</em> register instead, and is set out at the top of this page. The reasoning was
      sound and the conclusion was wrong, which is the ordinary way of it; the record of the wrong
      turn is left here because the next person may take the same one.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">A two-way conflict becomes a three-way one</h3>
      <p>The same parish record names <P n="Maria Nicotra" />'s mother as <strong>Venera
      Cosentino</strong>. The archive already held two readings: <strong>Monti Venera</strong> from
      the 1898 civil proclamation, and <strong>Venera Cassaneti</strong> from the Queensland death
      index. <strong>That is now three surnames for one woman</strong>, with only the given name
      <em>Venera</em> constant across all of them. <em>Cassaneti</em> and <em>Cosentino</em> may be
      the same word badly read twice. It stays unresolved, and it is worse than it was this
      morning.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Italy, Catania, Catania, Civil Registration
      (Tribunale), 1866–1929 — FamilySearch <code>1:1:XWTK-5R8Q</code>. Piedimonte Etneo parish
      register, Marriage Records 1777–1911 — <code>3:1:3QS7-99ZG-7XNZ</code>. Italy, Catania, Diocesi
      di Acireale, Catholic Church Records, 1560–1950.</p>
    </div>
  </section>



  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{M.total}</div><div class="l">people called Mazza in the tree</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{M.groups.length}</div><div class="l">separate descent groups</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{big}</div><div class="l">still joined to the family by nothing</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the name is</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><em>Mazza</em> is the Italian for a club, a mace or a mallet, from the Latin
      <em>mattea</em>. It became a surname in every available way at once: as a nickname for a strong
      or a violent man, as a trade name for someone who swung a maul, and — most commonly — from the
      medieval personal name <em>Mazzo</em>, a short form of Giacomazzo, Tommazzo and the like.</p>
      <p>It is among the most widespread surnames in Italy and it was <strong>coined independently,
      many times over</strong>, from Lombardy to Sicily. There is no founding Mazza family. That is
      not a disclaimer — it is the operative fact of this page, and it is why the six groups below
      are kept apart rather than drawn as one tree.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Group 1</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The line that reaches the children</h2>
    {spine.map((g) => (
      <div>
        <div class="meta" style="margin:1rem 0 1.2rem">
          <span><b>{g.n}</b> people</span>
          <span>{g.earliest || "····"}–{g.latest || "····"}</span>
          <span>{g.towns.join(" · ")}</span>
          <span>component {g.component}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="dek" style="max-width:80ch">Five generations and then it stops:
        <P n="Rosario Mazza" /> — of whom one single fact is known, that he fathered Salvatore —
        then <P n="Salvatore Mazza" /> born at Piedimonte Etneo in 1879, <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" />
        who sailed on the <em>Toscana</em> in 1949, and down to
        <strong>Mia</strong> and <strong>Rocco Francesco</strong>. It is the
        <a href={u("/lines/")}>shallowest of the four quarters</a> by five generations.</p>
        <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Dates</th><th>Born</th><th>On the spine</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{g.people.map((x) => (
              <tr>
                <td><a href={u("/people/" + x.slug)}>{x.name}</a></td>
                <td class="m">{x.datesHidden ? <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">withheld</span>
                  : ([x.born, x.died].filter(Boolean).join("–") || "—")}</td>
                <td>{x.birthPlace || "—"}</td>
                <td>{g.onSpine.includes(x.name) ? <span class="chip doc">ancestor</span> : ""}</td>
              </tr>))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Groups 2 to {M.groups.length}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The {big} who still share the name and nothing else</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">The big one is gone: the hundred and ten Piedimonte
    Etneo Mazza who once sat here unattached are now <a href={u("/the-hundred-and-ten/")}>part of group
    one</a>, proved the descendants of Salvatore's brothers by five register acts. What is left are
    fragments — a handful of people entered with no parent, spouse or child, who cannot be placed
    either way.</p>

    <div class="stack">
      {apart.map((g, i) => (
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:21px">Group {i + 2}
            <span class="mono" style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-3);margin-left:12px">
              {g.n} {g.n === 1 ? "person" : "people"} · component {g.component}
              {g.earliest ? ` · ${g.earliest}–${g.latest}` : " · undated"}
              {g.towns.length ? ` · ${g.towns[0]}` : ""}
            </span>
          </h3>
          {g.n > 3 ? (
            <div class="evlist" style="margin-top:12px">
              {g.people.map((x) => (
                <div><b>{x.born || "····"}</b><a href={u("/people/" + x.slug)}>{x.name}</a></div>
              ))}
            </div>
          ) : (
            <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;margin-top:8px;color:var(--ink-2)">
              {g.people.map((x, j) => (<>{j > 0 && " · "}<a href={u("/people/" + x.slug)}>{x.name}</a></>))}
              {" "}— a fragment with no parents and no children recorded, joined to nothing.
            </p>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Compare the Arena</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">Thirty-nine <a href={u("/arena/")}>Arena</a> in this tree
    form <strong>one single unbroken family</strong>, 1821 to 1937, all of Scilla. A hundred and
    twenty Mazza form six groups. That is not because the Arena were better recorded — it is because
    <em>Arena</em> arrived in this archive through one household that had been worked on, and
    <em>Mazza</em> arrived as a surname. <strong>The name a family is known by tells you least about
    it.</strong></p>
  </section>
</Base>
